  "step_2": "The individual's verbal report states that the difference in the probability of a loss is very small, and therefore they would choose the option with the smaller absolute loss. The individual focuses on the magnitude of the loss ('geringeren Verlust') and considers the probabilities of loss as negligible. They are comparing the sizes of the potential losses (-6000 vs -3000) and prefer the smaller loss, implying a focus on minimizing the worst-case outcome. The justification is based on loss magnitude and near-equality of risk, not on deviations from an aspiration level or evaluation of outcomes relative to a computed average. The individual does not mention any reference point derived from averaging outcomes, nor do they discuss positive deviations from such a point.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires computing an aspiration level as the mean of all outcomes and then evaluating which lottery has a higher chance of exceeding this level (i.e., yielding a positive difference). The aspiration level (-2250) and the positive difference (only for outcome 0) are central to the reason. However, the individual does not reference such a calculation or any concept of deviation from an average outcome. Instead, the individual focuses on the absolute size of the loss and downplays the small difference in probabilities. Their reasoning aligns more with a minimax or regret-averse heuristic, preferring the less severe loss when risks are perceived as similar. There is no indication that the individual computed or used an aspiration level or assessed outcomes based on whether they are above or below it. Therefore, the information and justification in the verbal report do not align with the decision reason.",
